<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asiatic Vespers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/88-bce">88 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Ephesian Vespers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Asiatic Vespers was the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Italians in the province of Asia and neighbouring regions ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> in 88 BCE, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war">First Mithridatic War</a>. Ancient writers put the number of dead at 80,000 or more. Many Greek cities, resentful of Roman tax-farmers and moneylenders, carried out the order, and the massacre made the war a bitter struggle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asiatic-vespers.jpg" alt="Asiatic Vespers" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilya Yakubovich · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1stMithritadicwar89BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the years before the massacre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI Eupator</a> of Pontus extended his power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and with Nicomedes III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> he partitioned Paphlagonia in 108–107 BCE despite a Roman embassy's protest. Disputes over Cappadocia followed. Mithridates installed his own son as Ariarathes IX about 101 BCE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later ordered both kings out of the country and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a>, then governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, to install Ariobarzanes I.</p><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was occupied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>, Mithridates allied with Tigranes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and drove the kings of Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> from their thrones. A Roman commission under Manius Aquillius restored them, and the restored kings, unable to pay Rome's bill for their restoration, were encouraged to invade Pontus. Mithridates answered by invading Cappadocia and Bithynia, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv">Nicomedes IV</a> and Aquillius, and by the middle of 89 BCE had conquered most of Roman Asia. Rome declared war that autumn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The killings were a calculated reply to the Roman declaration of war, meant to bind the cities to Mithridates, since no city that took part could hope to be received back into Roman allegiance. He wrote secretly to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> and to local leaders, ordering them to kill all Italian residents, with their wives, children and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of Italian birth, thirty days after the date of his letter. Slaves who informed on their Italian masters were offered freedom, debtors who killed their creditors relief from debt, and killers and informers half the property of the dead.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, Adramyttion, Caunus, Tralles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> and the island of Chios all saw atrocities. Valerius Maximus and Memnon give about 80,000 dead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, less credibly, 150,000, while fragments of Dio suggest that the reported numbers are exaggerated. The precise date is disputed; Ernst Badian, judging precision impossible, placed the massacre in the first half of 88 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> received the command against Mithridates, but a Roman assembly transferred it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, and Sulla marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> before crossing the Adriatic with few troops and no heavy warships. Meanwhile a Pontic fleet cleared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Mithridates won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> through his partisans there, and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> held out against him. By the time Sulla's men besieged Athens, mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> had rallied to Rome. The name Ephesian Vespers was coined in 1890 by the historian Théodore Reinach, after the Sicilian Vespers of 1282, and some scholars consider the massacres a case of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asiatic Vespers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mithridates’ order in 88 BCE to kill the Romans and Italians in Asia, the so-called <b>Asiatic Vespers</b>, in which ancient sources claim that 80,000 or more people died, became notorious, and the welcome he received as a liberator in Greek cities such as Athens showed how resented Roman…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
